.TH BIGINTSHIFTLEFT 3 "2026-09-25" "BigNums 3" "Library Functions Manual"
.nr HY 0
.ds AD l
.SH NAME
bigIntShiftLeft, bigIntShiftRight, bigIntGetBit \- shift or inspect integer bits
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <bignums.h>

int bigIntShiftLeft(BigInt *a, int64_t bits);
int bigIntShiftRight(BigInt *a, int64_t bits);
int bigIntGetBit(const BigInt *a, size_t bit_index);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Shifts use int64_t counts. Negative counts reverse direction, including
INT64_MIN without signed-negation overflow. Left shift grows storage exactly;
right shift discards low magnitude bits and truncates toward zero. For example,
-7 shifted right one bit gives -3, not -4. Nonzero results preserve the sign;
shifting beyond all bits to the right yields canonical positive zero.
Shifting zero allocates nothing. A zero count has no effect.
GetBit uses a size_t index from the least significant magnitude bit; indices
beyond the magnitude return zero. This is not a two's-complement representation.
There is no signed-negative index interface.
.SH RETURN VALUE
Shifts return 0, -1 for allocation failure or INT_MAX for an unrepresentable
size. Errors leave the value unchanged. GetBit returns 0 or 1.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bignums (3),
.BR bignums (7)
